449
00:36:15,499 --> 00:36:23,088
I mean, can we have a Friends of the Human Rights Commission group, you know, for people who want to support their community?

450
00:36:24,730 --> 00:36:27,313
So just throwing all that out there.

451
00:36:27,994 --> 00:36:32,158
But the recommendations will come from the current commissioners.

452
00:36:32,719 --> 00:36:35,301
I have zero voting authority.

453
00:36:36,202 --> 00:36:41,568
Maybe that's a good thing because otherwise I would say I would love every single person to join us.

454
00:36:42,709 --> 00:36:45,452
But just wanted to share that with you.

455
00:36:46,627 --> 00:36:51,970
Now, I'm gonna turn it over to Munir and maybe, Judy, or is it just you, Munir?

456
00:36:52,851 --> 00:36:56,233
Is it too early to give an update on Safe MedMed?

457
00:36:56,293 --> 00:37:01,796
Maybe talk about why they got involved and where they are now, if you're comfortable.

458
00:37:01,956 --> 00:37:04,137
No, yeah, I'll speak briefly to that.

459
00:37:04,177 --> 00:37:11,061
Just to, I mean, what an amazing group of people that have connected here.

460
00:37:11,201 --> 00:37:11,701
And my hope is that

461
00:37:15,768 --> 00:37:27,755
since some of you will not be officially commissioners, but as Frances said, maybe be friends of the HRC and be active that way.

462
00:37:28,635 --> 00:37:40,882
But briefly, just to fill you all in, there is right now an active effort committee that was

463
00:37:42,652 --> 00:38:03,857
at least the way things developed, is that we initially had a subcommittee of the Human Rights Commission that was going to look at the Human Rights Commission and at the ordinance and see how we can perhaps improve it a little bit or improve it a lot.

464
00:38:06,438 --> 00:38:20,309
And we worked with a group from an organization, Safe Medford, two members of whom are right here, Steve and Judy.

465
00:38:20,389 --> 00:38:24,352
Judy is a commissioner as well as myself.

466
00:38:25,550 --> 00:38:51,081
And so we initially worked on the subcommittee, including both HRC and members of Safe Medford, but then subsequently we decided that it was easier to officially withdraw the Human Rights Commission subcommittee out of this committee.

467
00:38:51,777 --> 00:39:04,521
because of restrictions that basically were involved in the open meeting law and it restricted our access to people and so on and so forth.

468
00:39:05,381 --> 00:39:19,506
So basically this subcommittee is now actively at work, has been for several months, and we've interviewed a lot of folks who were previously connected to the Human Rights Commission,

469
00:39:20,391 --> 00:39:26,334
we will be bringing some recommendations to the Human Rights Commission.

470
00:39:27,415 --> 00:39:42,263
Hopefully in about a month, maybe two, Judy and Steve can correct me if they think this is not quite practical.

471
00:39:43,088 --> 00:39:57,880
But, and that's, you know, we have, you know, we're quite excited about this and looking forward to, you know, building a very active and involved Human Rights Commission.

472
00:39:58,640 --> 00:40:00,242
Judy, you wanna add something to that?

473
00:40:01,763 --> 00:40:10,370
No, if anybody has any questions, you know, we'd all be happy to speak to that.

474
00:40:12,092 --> 00:40:13,672
Any questions about this?

475
00:40:14,233 --> 00:40:22,595
It's a committee of the group Safe Medford now, and I think everything else has been said.

476
00:40:22,995 --> 00:40:24,875
Steve, do you want to take it?

477
00:40:25,656 --> 00:40:26,016
Thank you.

478
00:40:26,056 --> 00:40:36,158
The only thing that hasn't been said, I just want to make clear, some of you may know, Safe Medford is about six or seven years old, I believe.

479
00:40:36,938 --> 00:40:38,879
It actually came about

480
00:40:40,635 --> 00:41:00,101
out of a project called Medford Community Conversations, the Medford Conversations Project, which tried to get groups of regular residents and employees in Medford to talk about key issues in small group facilitated discussions.

481
00:41:00,841 --> 00:41:04,162
And one of the first outcomes of some of those discussions was the plight

482
00:41:09,278 --> 00:41:16,944
of immigrants, particularly undocumented immigrants, given the political climate at the time.

483
00:41:17,244 --> 00:41:22,328
It was just before the 2016 election.

484
00:41:23,288 --> 00:41:28,292
And folks felt, immigrants in particular, others as well, were unsafe in Medford.

485
00:41:28,832 --> 00:41:32,695
And this group formed to see what it can do.

486
00:41:33,195 --> 00:41:35,257
Since then, it has broadened its mandate

487
00:41:36,772 --> 00:41:46,994
to just say we're focused on making Medford safe and secure and thriving for all residents, regardless of status or group or anything else.

488
00:41:48,195 --> 00:41:49,475
So that's a broader mandate.

489
00:41:49,715 --> 00:42:04,758
And we have seen the HRC as a great vehicle for addressing human rights violations or pushing a human rights agenda that includes safety and security for all its residents.

490
00:42:05,645 --> 00:42:11,031
And we agreed to help out the HRC.

491
00:42:11,791 --> 00:42:19,579
And as Munir and Judy said, to develop some recommendations for your consideration.

492
00:42:24,705 --> 00:42:26,967
Thank you so much, Steve.

493
00:42:30,427 --> 00:42:32,028
Frances, can I jump in for a second?

494
00:42:32,488 --> 00:42:54,937
I kind of just want to lift, I think for me, it feels really special that we're toeing the line of having the HRC with, you know, the gift of the city being here with us between Chief Buckley and having Frances here and having that, establishing that connection, but also pairing that with the freedom of something like Safe Medford and being able to have more room

495
00:42:55,657 --> 00:42:59,799
in terms of the working conversations that get advanced and choosing your own timelines and goals.

496
00:43:00,759 --> 00:43:15,886
So I think for me, I'm kind of on that hamster wheel that Frances mentioned, I'm supposed to be on a different Zoom meeting right now, but I think for me, the titles mean very little and the goal is really about pushing the racial equity and innovation and vision.

497
00:43:16,106 --> 00:43:24,970
And so all of our meetings are open and I would love and encourage all of you to sort of join and contribute and share what is coming up for you irrespective of

498
00:43:26,231 --> 00:43:32,334
titles and whatnot, because your energy is important and welcomed and respected.

499
00:43:33,035 --> 00:43:42,981
So yeah, I am appreciative of all of you and I apologize that I need to jump off, but a mother's work is never done and I'm supposed to talk to the principal.

500
00:43:43,261 --> 00:43:46,843
And I have new kids in the Medford Public Schools.

501
00:43:46,903 --> 00:43:49,965
So yeah, good luck to all of us, I guess.

502
00:43:50,906 --> 00:43:52,026
Thank you, Shelly.

503
00:43:52,226 --> 00:43:52,747
Good luck.

504
00:43:53,447 --> 00:43:54,268
Thank you, guys.

505
00:43:55,958 --> 00:43:59,041
All right, so good to see faces new and old.

506
00:43:59,321 --> 00:44:00,382
Have a lovely night, everybody.

507
00:44:05,426 --> 00:44:09,289
So we're moving ahead in our agenda.

508
00:44:10,651 --> 00:44:15,314
Commissioners, I just wanted to find out, did you have anything else to add?

509
00:44:15,395 --> 00:44:23,682
Because I would like to give the opportunity for our invited guests to ask questions that

510
00:44:24,887 --> 00:44:37,072
you know, they might have or of each person or just general question that somebody can take so that we can have a two-way dialogue.

511
00:44:42,134 --> 00:44:45,055
Am I going to have to voluntold my commissions?

512
00:44:48,057 --> 00:44:49,177
I didn't drink coffee.

513
00:44:49,197 --> 00:44:49,977
I was good.

514
00:44:50,077 --> 00:44:50,858
I went to tea.

515
00:44:56,013 --> 00:44:59,454
All right, so maybe I'll turn it over to our invited guests.

516
00:44:59,534 --> 00:45:09,837
I mean, you know, are there areas of the Human Rights Commission that you would like to know more about or specific roles, specific functions, thoughts, ideas?

517
00:45:10,638 --> 00:45:14,659
I'm open, I'm open to the dialogue and we all are.

518
00:45:18,412 --> 00:45:32,342
So, so as someone completely new to this, I would just love to understand how the arc of the year will work and are there already established goals and like, what are the plans for the work in the year ahead?

519
00:45:32,462 --> 00:45:41,669
And will we, will we be divvied up into little working teams that will we, and we'll be reporting back to each other monthly?

520
00:45:41,689 --> 00:45:46,052
I think I'm just looking to understand how, how we will all work together.

521
00:45:46,730 --> 00:45:48,051
throughout the year ahead?

522
00:45:51,813 --> 00:45:57,697
Let me put in a couple of words that might address that.

523
00:46:00,579 --> 00:46:10,445
It might behoove you all to take a look at the current ordinance that established the Human Rights Commission.

524
00:46:12,586 --> 00:46:15,568
It's been a while since it's been looked at.

525
00:46:17,150 --> 00:46:35,004
And that's one of the reasons that this subcommittee from Safe Medford is going to be recommending all sorts of things and try to make it more active and more relevant to our times.

526
00:46:36,325 --> 00:46:44,572
So as far as the details of how the Human Rights Commission works,

527
00:46:45,405 --> 00:46:49,166
that's pretty much left to the Human Rights Commission itself.

528
00:46:50,847 --> 00:47:04,352
We have our monthly meetings, as you probably know, but beyond that, there are projects that the Human Rights Commission have worked on in the past.

529
00:47:05,813 --> 00:47:11,135
Now the pandemic has impacted us significantly, I would say.

530
00:47:12,907 --> 00:47:23,295
because of difficulties in having face-to-face contact, although we're slowly getting back to some sort of normalcy.

531
00:47:24,491 --> 00:47:47,015
Uh, but, uh, you know, so there will be, I guess, subcommittees, uh, and hopefully the recommendations that, uh, that will be coming forward from this subcommittee, uh, will, uh, will meet the approval of, uh, this commission.

532
00:47:48,202 --> 00:48:02,213
And then, of course, the next step will be to contact the city itself, because I guess the ordinance is through the city council, and I'm sure the administration will be involved.

533
00:48:03,033 --> 00:48:06,996
So we'll have some work ahead of us, I'm sure, to set things right.

534
00:48:07,097 --> 00:48:12,060
And then we'll have our priorities and see how we can advance

535
00:48:16,383 --> 00:48:27,050
the human rights of all people throughout all the departments in the city and throughout all the organizations in the city.

536
00:48:27,970 --> 00:48:33,894
So I don't know if that answers your question, but that's just in generalities my thinking on this.

537
00:48:33,914 --> 00:48:37,416
That's very helpful.

538
00:48:37,476 --> 00:48:37,876
Thank you.

539
00:48:40,172 --> 00:48:40,533
Thank you.

540
00:48:40,593 --> 00:48:44,541
We know where the ordinance is available for people.

541
00:48:44,641 --> 00:48:49,231
And by the way, it's extremely out of date.

542
00:48:50,340 --> 00:48:53,301
Yes, that's why it is Judy.

543
00:48:54,061 --> 00:48:55,321
And I've got good news.

544
00:48:55,461 --> 00:49:09,365
I didn't, I didn't share, but I actually had someone retype it so that there is a word version versus a PDF that nobody can edit that prints out, you know, all wonky.

545
00:49:09,785 --> 00:49:17,827
But I did have it retyped so that that way, when we are ready to move forward as a group, everybody can have a copy.

546
00:49:18,467 --> 00:49:18,807
right?

547
00:49:19,168 --> 00:49:21,929
Some people like to print out and write their notes.

548
00:49:22,310 --> 00:49:25,091
Some people like to make their edits on the computer.

549
00:49:26,012 --> 00:49:33,757
But there is a Word version of it now, which I'm hoping will make it easier for us.

550
00:49:35,518 --> 00:49:37,380
So how old is the current version?

551
00:49:37,600 --> 00:49:38,180
I'm curious.

552
00:49:39,961 --> 00:49:42,443
I think it's probably around 30 years old.

553
00:49:44,643 --> 00:49:45,784
Judy, do you know?

554
00:49:46,164 --> 00:49:47,665
I really don't.

555
00:49:47,805 --> 00:49:48,526
I really don't.

556
00:49:48,626 --> 00:49:54,129
But it's also the human rights ordinance for the city.

557
00:49:55,189 --> 00:49:58,832
So it establishes the city's commitment to human rights.

558
00:49:58,952 --> 00:50:05,475
But yeah, I don't know how old it is.

559
00:50:05,495 --> 00:50:09,278
There's one pretty hard to read PDF.

560
00:50:09,318 --> 00:50:11,079
I actually do have a Word version.

561
00:50:17,152 --> 00:50:22,473
Is the one that was posted to the site as updated in 2015 not much of an update?

562
00:50:23,514 --> 00:50:27,394
So there's one on the website that was posted as an update in 2015.

563
00:50:27,454 --> 00:50:31,435
Was that not much of an update and still considered updated?

564
00:50:33,035 --> 00:50:36,876
So the one that I have is a signed copy.

565
00:50:36,896 --> 00:50:38,136
It was signed December 16, 2014.

566
00:50:41,733 --> 00:50:43,555
2000 what?

567
00:50:44,175 --> 00:50:43,555
14.

568
00:50:44,195 --> 00:50:43,555
14.

569
00:50:45,276 --> 00:50:46,117
14.

570
00:50:45,736 --> 00:50:46,117
Yeah.

571
00:50:46,177 --> 00:50:48,959
So revised one on the website.

572
00:50:50,320 --> 00:50:52,061
I don't know if that's a revised one.

573
00:50:52,101 --> 00:50:53,482
It's probably the same one.

574
00:50:53,542 --> 00:50:55,364
It's more than likely the same one.

575
00:50:56,485 --> 00:51:02,229
Because by the time it goes for signature, then it gets sent out, approved and then posted.

576
00:51:02,249 --> 00:51:02,329
Yeah.

577
00:51:05,887 --> 00:51:24,375
So I mean, again, I think that with things like this, you know, whether any group, any committee, it's always good to have a look see at least once every five years, because things change right in 2018, you know, you know, there was really no advancement in zoom.

578
00:51:25,055 --> 00:51:27,917
Webex, Teams, things of that nature.

579
00:51:28,357 --> 00:51:32,839
Those of us that lived internationally were celebrating the use of WhatsApp.

580
00:51:33,239 --> 00:51:43,885
We could make free phone calls, we didn't have to have those calling cards, you know, and then the big advancement was that you could actually load your calling card into the phone itself.

581
00:51:44,705 --> 00:51:50,408
So you could auto load it if you ran out of funds instead of running out to go buy another one and scratch it off.

582
00:51:51,048 --> 00:51:53,109
So look at where we are now.

583
00:51:53,529 --> 00:51:58,491
I mean, even with Zoom, look at all the different reaction buttons that Zoom has.

584
00:51:58,551 --> 00:52:01,473
Zoom did not have that at the start of the pandemic.

585
00:52:02,953 --> 00:52:04,374
You know, they did not have

586
00:52:05,682 --> 00:52:13,929
The AI technology that does the audio translation where we get to see it in readable format.

587
00:52:14,410 --> 00:52:18,554
Teams was not even something that Microsoft had.

588
00:52:19,134 --> 00:52:20,435
You know, it was still Skype.

589
00:52:20,635 --> 00:52:24,239
So I think it's great that we look at these things.

590
00:52:24,319 --> 00:52:28,322
It's part of helping to drive this beautiful city.

591
00:52:29,043 --> 00:52:33,387
So we've got two hands and I want to say that I think it went Rebecca and then Jacqueline.

592
00:52:34,087 --> 00:52:36,790
And if I'm wrong, please switch the order.

593
00:52:39,332 --> 00:52:39,732
Thank you.

594
00:52:39,792 --> 00:52:51,102
Yeah, I was just wondering if commissioners could share a project or example of an initiative that you've been particularly proud of during your term as a commissioner on the Human Rights Commission.

595
00:52:51,122 --> 00:52:52,603
It'd be great to hear about that.

596
00:52:58,003 --> 00:53:02,006
I'm happy to jump in there and maybe this can provide some additional context.

597
00:53:02,046 --> 00:53:12,591
This isn't a formal project or process, but I think maybe it can shed some light into how the group has evolved even just since I've been involved.

598
00:53:13,712 --> 00:53:22,477
And really over the last, it's already been mentioned that we face some challenges during the pandemic with meeting and creating momentum and that sort of thing.

599
00:53:23,057 --> 00:53:23,898
And I think that

600
00:53:24,757 --> 00:53:34,139
even to say that we were able to keep the commission a functioning entity within the broader ecosystem of the city, was somewhat of an accomplishment throughout that time.

601
00:53:34,759 --> 00:53:41,761
So we've certainly had a number of things that we've done, discussed, put together.

602
00:53:41,801 --> 00:53:45,762
We've been able to bring content to different events, have representation to different events.

603
00:53:46,762 --> 00:53:51,523
But I think that everybody on the line here definitely deserves kudos for sticking with the group.

604
00:53:51,543 --> 00:53:54,184
There's been a lot of change over the last two and a half years.

605
00:53:54,671 --> 00:54:03,235
And the fact that we're all here now with almost a completely full screen of folks interested to join with us is something that I can say that I'm proud of for sure.

606
00:54:08,258 --> 00:54:10,359
I mean, I can add a little bit to that.

607
00:54:10,379 --> 00:54:21,044
I mean, traditionally, the Human Rights Commission has always worked on the Martin Luther King celebration.

608
00:54:22,284 --> 00:54:23,705
And we've done that.

609
00:54:24,112 --> 00:54:28,676
before the pandemic where everybody was helping out in person.

610
00:54:29,577 --> 00:54:38,165
And then we tried to do that sort of, you know, work on that during the pandemic, although it was a challenge.

611
00:54:39,546 --> 00:54:45,772
We've also, during the pandemic, there was a large

612
00:54:47,340 --> 00:55:00,626
City, whole city meeting that the Human Rights Commission kind of co-sponsored with the city to have a conversation about discrimination in Medford.

613
00:55:01,706 --> 00:55:10,190
And that involved a meeting of about, there were like 240 people on Zoom in that meeting.

614
00:55:10,410 --> 00:55:14,492
I don't know if any of you were, happened to be in that meeting.

615
00:55:15,091 --> 00:55:31,955
But that was an incredible discussion where people were split into 15 rooms and they discussed various issues that some of the people there had experienced.

616
00:55:33,115 --> 00:55:44,058
And we spent the next several months in the Human Rights Commission trying to highlight those issues and bring discussion to see how

617
00:55:45,367 --> 00:55:49,169
and if we were able to address them or if the city were able to address them.

618
00:55:49,589 --> 00:55:52,610
So these were just a couple of things that come to mind.

619
00:55:59,113 --> 00:56:00,073
Thank you, Munir.

620
00:56:00,253 --> 00:56:02,774
And no worries, Jamie, got your message.

621
00:56:02,834 --> 00:56:04,235
Thank you for joining us.

622
00:56:04,935 --> 00:56:08,777
All right, Jacqueline and then Johnny.

623
00:56:11,618 --> 00:56:11,918
Thank you.

624
00:56:12,661 --> 00:56:15,365
My question is more around just like accessibility.

625
00:56:16,167 --> 00:56:18,852
During the pandemic sort of you mentioned some of the challenges.

626
00:56:20,109 --> 00:56:22,871
especially kind of, you know, going all remote.

627
00:56:23,011 --> 00:56:35,858
How have you sort of thought about human rights when thinking about, you know, some of those people that might have challenges getting onto a Zoom, sort of, you know, those older folks, or, you know, maybe they don't have accessibility to a computer.

628
00:56:35,898 --> 00:56:42,782
How have you worked through some of those challenges and been able to engage with them in the past couple of years?

629
00:56:47,205 --> 00:56:49,066
Frankly, we've been remiss on that.

630
00:56:54,032 --> 00:56:55,454
But you're right to point it out.

631
00:56:57,816 --> 00:57:00,439
So, and accessibility is a really big thing.

632
00:57:00,499 --> 00:57:10,830
I can't speak to how it was done before, but I know that these recordings, right, actually get played on channel 22.

633
00:57:13,360 --> 00:57:13,860
And channel 43.

634
00:57:16,662 --> 00:57:18,983
I think that I believe those are the two channels.

635
00:57:19,923 --> 00:57:21,324
Maybe I've reversed the numbers.

636
00:57:21,944 --> 00:57:28,627
So there is that opportunity for people who may still be reliant on television, right?

637
00:57:29,085 --> 00:57:30,506
to get their information.

638
00:57:30,747 --> 00:57:32,528
Again, it's not real time.

639
00:57:33,029 --> 00:57:42,317
So when we get to the stage where we say, okay, open for public participation, still, those individuals are left behind.

640
00:57:42,677 --> 00:57:45,760
And I think it's a conversation that we have to

641
00:57:46,618 --> 00:57:49,841
keep having, we have to keep engaging in.

642
00:57:51,783 --> 00:57:54,546
The whole another population that's left behind, right?

643
00:57:54,947 --> 00:58:06,418
Population that does not utilize spoken language, utilizes a visual language, do not read English the same way that we do.

644
00:58:08,574 --> 00:58:15,456
still need to have the conversation to continue to increase broadening and meeting the need.

645
00:58:15,816 --> 00:58:24,039
And it's not just with the Human Rights Commission, it's with, you know, who we say we are as a city when we really talk about creating that space.

646
00:58:24,059 --> 00:58:25,499
I can't remember who said it.

647
00:58:25,859 --> 00:58:34,042
Someone said it so well, that space where you come to Medford and you just know, yeah, you belong, no questions asked.

648
00:58:34,102 --> 00:58:36,623
I can't remember who said it.

649
00:58:38,224 --> 00:58:40,025
earlier on during the introduction.

650
00:58:40,945 --> 00:58:43,706
That's really what belonging means.

651
00:58:44,106 --> 00:58:46,127
Belonging is different from inclusion.

652
00:58:46,647 --> 00:58:50,768
Belonging is not having to worry about, can I go to that store?

653
00:58:50,808 --> 00:58:53,229
Can I go to that restaurant?

654
00:58:53,289 --> 00:59:01,051
Can I ride my mobile device down on the sidewalk, even though we have outdoor dining?

655
00:59:01,371 --> 00:59:03,452
It's never having to think about those things.

656
00:59:04,155 --> 00:59:08,560
But just knowing that you can do it, because that space is created for you.

657
00:59:11,283 --> 00:59:14,807
All right, so we got Johnny and Merland.

658
00:59:17,610 --> 00:59:17,810
Yeah.

659
00:59:20,934 --> 00:59:27,577
Many of us who live in Medford may work elsewhere, and many people who live elsewhere work in Medford.

660
00:59:29,058 --> 00:59:35,821
The city of Medford is connected to the surrounding towns and other cities.

661
00:59:36,301 --> 00:59:40,563
I wondered whether there are other commissions in other towns or cities, or is there any?

662
00:59:40,583 --> 00:59:50,707
Do you come together with other groups from other cities and towns and talk about the issues which affect all of us or not?

663
00:59:52,631 --> 00:59:57,676
I can address that because I've had some experience with that, although recently I haven't been part of it.

664
00:59:58,296 --> 01:00:09,346
But there is a group, which is the Mass Human Rights Commission, and they meet once a month.

665
01:00:09,866 --> 01:00:12,008
Again, we used to meet in person.

666
01:00:12,028 --> 01:00:15,111
As a matter of fact, we used to meet right here in Medford.

667
01:00:17,968 --> 01:00:36,932
but then during the pandemic it's been on Zoom and they typically meet and discuss their activities on the second Friday of each month at 9 a.m.

668
01:00:38,713 --> 01:00:44,234
and if anyone is interested and everybody is welcome to

669
01:00:44,933 --> 01:00:49,357
you know, join in and participate in these meetings.

670
01:00:50,097 --> 01:01:03,529
If anyone is interested, I can send the link, usually the Zoom link, I can send it to Frances and, you know, let Frances know that you're interested.

671
01:01:06,031 --> 01:01:10,875
The Medford Human Rights Commission has not been actively

672
01:01:12,644 --> 01:01:18,949
involved in those meetings recently, I would say in the past year and a half.

673
01:01:19,709 --> 01:01:30,297
I just, you know, occasionally drop in, but because of my grand children duties, it's a tough time for me to meet at 9am.

674
01:01:31,827 --> 01:01:39,213
on Friday, so I usually just access their notes, but they have a large mailing list and they mail it to everybody.

675
01:01:39,873 --> 01:01:43,876
So that's the long winding answer to your question.

676
01:01:44,637 --> 01:01:45,117
Thanks.

677
01:01:46,478 --> 01:01:49,580
Thank you so much for providing that information, Muneer.

678
01:01:50,101 --> 01:01:59,468
In the chat, I have put the link, and for those who are not able to view chat, it is www,

679
01:02:01,720 --> 01:02:08,021
www.mahrc.info.

680
01:02:09,302 --> 01:02:17,564
So www.mahrc.info.

681
01:02:17,644 --> 01:02:23,065
That will take you directly to the coalitions page.

682
01:02:28,715 --> 01:02:29,456
All right.

683
01:02:29,936 --> 01:02:31,718
Chief, you look like you want to say something.

684
01:02:33,380 --> 01:02:34,481
I was just about to call them.

685
01:02:35,002 --> 01:02:42,149
I do, but I didn't know if you wanted to call and you can you can ask a question and I'll I'll respond.

686
01:02:42,709 --> 01:02:43,070
OK.

687
01:02:43,210 --> 01:02:44,091
All right.

688
01:02:44,251 --> 01:02:45,072
Please go ahead.

689
01:02:45,172 --> 01:02:45,592
Sorry.

690
01:02:46,793 --> 01:02:47,314
All righty.

691
01:02:47,394 --> 01:02:48,395
Thank you, everyone.

692
01:02:48,415 --> 01:02:51,318
So my question was very similar to Johnny.

693
01:02:52,648 --> 01:03:00,029
really what other organization are we involved with within the other cities and also, you know, at a state level as well.

694
01:03:01,010 --> 01:03:13,652
And second piece to the question will be, you know, in what instance should a resident, you know, expect to see, like, the Human Rights Commission involved?

695
01:03:14,272 --> 01:03:19,453
You know, and, you know, I'm just trying to understand in what instance, you know,

696
01:03:20,091 --> 01:03:26,773
such an organization like that will be called and then what cases will be evaluated and stuff like that.

697
01:03:32,855 --> 01:03:36,356
Anybody want to take that or are you deferring?

698
01:03:39,337 --> 01:03:41,017
Judy, did you chuckle at me?

699
01:03:41,217 --> 01:03:44,358
I don't think I understand the question.

700
01:03:45,419 --> 01:03:46,179
The second part.

701
01:03:47,414 --> 01:03:52,037
The second piece is that, you know, as a resident of Medford, right?

702
01:03:52,117 --> 01:03:54,999
We know this organization exists, right?

703
01:03:56,539 --> 01:04:00,422
And some residents like me probably don't know it even exists.

704
01:04:00,662 --> 01:04:09,547
What I'm trying to say, in what circumstances should a resident be expected to see or have the Human Rights Commission involved?

705
01:04:10,527 --> 01:04:11,108
Involved?

706
01:04:11,708 --> 01:04:11,928
Yes.

707
01:04:12,628 --> 01:04:13,409
In one instance.

708
01:04:16,815 --> 01:04:26,437
I think that's a challenge that we have to face in the coming months, starting with a report from the St.

709
01:04:26,477 --> 01:04:27,298
Bedford Committee.

710
01:04:27,858 --> 01:04:29,258
I think it's true.

711
01:04:29,298 --> 01:04:32,819
We don't necessarily have a presence in the community.

712
01:04:33,479 --> 01:04:42,361
When there used to be a lot of in-person events, we would have a table.

713
01:04:47,767 --> 01:05:01,103
You know, there are some now and there are some in-person events, but yeah, I think that our visibility in the community is a serious challenge and

714
01:05:07,657 --> 01:05:11,338
It's almost as if the... I think that the whole... Right.

715
01:05:11,718 --> 01:05:17,459
The whole commission will need to really... Oops, sorry, that was an accident.

716
01:05:17,999 --> 01:05:22,760
Will really need to be thinking about that.

717
01:05:24,961 --> 01:05:26,721
What do people know about us?

718
01:05:27,301 --> 01:05:30,702
Oh, a lot of people don't know about us.

719
01:05:36,617 --> 01:05:37,697
Judy, did I, did we lose you?

720
01:05:37,718 --> 01:05:44,461
And how would we make our presence known in a way that fulfills our mission?

721
01:05:44,601 --> 01:06:05,250
I mean, we should be very visible, I think, in the community, but that is, you know, we have to think through the operationally, you know, how do we make ourselves visible?

722
01:06:07,052 --> 01:06:09,112
So I think that's a good question.

723
01:06:11,213 --> 01:06:19,975
I think also clarifying for people what it means to be a human rights commission for a municipality or even a state level.

724
01:06:20,715 --> 01:06:30,557
You know, people, many people hear human rights and some of the calls I receive really more or less belong to organizations like Amnesty International.

725
01:06:32,064 --> 01:06:46,786
So it's, yes, we have the ordinance, but a nice, simple, clarifying statement could go a long way to help us create our identity.

726
01:06:48,429 --> 01:06:56,093
And to help people better understand who the Human Rights Commission is for Medford and what the Human Rights Commission does.

727
01:06:56,593 --> 01:06:59,714
And it's funny because the Human Rights Commission actually does a lot.

728
01:07:00,115 --> 01:07:09,819
Now remember, I've only been here in March, but I can actually name all the different things that members of the commission supported in the six months that I've been here.

729
01:07:12,498 --> 01:07:22,905
But it may not appear that way, because it's not like, oh, there's a banner, or t-shirts, or swag, or things of that nature.

730
01:07:28,549 --> 01:07:29,289
Thank you.

731
01:07:29,449 --> 01:07:35,113
Any of the commissioners want to take that, or our honorary commissioner?

732
01:07:35,193 --> 01:07:37,155
Steve, I feel like you're an honorary commissioner.

733
01:07:38,512 --> 01:07:41,533
I want to add to that, chief.

734
01:07:41,713 --> 01:07:57,421
Well, I think I'd like to just add a few comments here that first thing that I'm really excited about is that we have nine people who've come tonight to the to have us express their interest in being part of the Human Rights Commission and additionally.

735
01:07:59,105 --> 01:08:03,231
kind of the thing that excites me more than is the ideas and the questions that you're asking.

736
01:08:03,792 --> 01:08:13,826
So to be honest with you, you ask a lot of good questions and we pause and we hesitate we spent a large part of the past year, struggling with what our identity is as a human rights commission.

737
01:08:14,627 --> 01:08:24,691
and trying to define our role, our place, even as nitty gritty as, you know, how do we break up into subcommittees and without violating open meeting laws.

738
01:08:25,591 --> 01:08:35,255
And it's been a small group and in my four years now, I don't think we've ever had nine commissioners, we have the ability now to reach out to that and, and change this and

739
01:08:36,575 --> 01:08:53,793
You're, you represent that change to us coming here tonight, and that's what I'm really happy about and even though we can't, you know, rattle off a whole bunch of a lot of the things, the hope is that what this is the first step to making the human rights, more visible, more actionable sort of organization in a group.

740
01:08:54,493 --> 01:08:57,657
And so there's a lot of hope in what you bring to us tonight.

741
01:08:58,118 --> 01:09:04,305
And we are a commission that's working hard and trying to do certain things, but maybe we're not as visible as we need.

742
01:09:04,686 --> 01:09:07,749
I'm telling you, we've recognized that and we're trying to find solutions to that.

743
01:09:08,090 --> 01:09:11,814
And the hope is that you can be on the ground floor of changing that with us.

744
01:09:13,109 --> 01:09:18,093
And so I think by way of that may help answer some of the questions that we're looking for.

745
01:09:18,113 --> 01:09:23,758
We're hoping for a new Human Rights Commission, some change and some excitement moving forward.

746
01:09:23,798 --> 01:09:30,103
And if you have that energy and you want to be part of it, we're very, very happy to see you all here tonight.

747
01:09:30,123 --> 01:09:34,126
So I know they didn't answer a specific question, but I thought it would just be important to say.

748
01:09:38,609 --> 01:09:39,610
Well said, Jack.

749
01:09:41,050 --> 01:10:00,100
One thought that comes to my mind at least is that there is always the possibility of requesting that the number of commissioners be much larger than nine, in which case a lot more people can get involved.

750
01:10:02,681 --> 01:10:03,421
Yeah, absolutely.

751
01:10:06,483 --> 01:10:07,263
Thank you, Chief.

752
01:10:07,363 --> 01:10:08,684
Thank you, Muneer, Steve.

753
01:10:09,770 --> 01:10:15,772
Just quickly, I really support what Chief Buckley and Munir has just said.

754
01:10:15,792 --> 01:10:29,038
This is an interesting time for so many committed folks to express interest in joining the commission.

755
01:10:30,038 --> 01:10:34,700
Quite frankly, it's been a difficult period during the pandemic.

756
01:10:36,797 --> 01:10:42,278
for the commission to do its work for a variety of reasons, the pandemic being a big one, but there were others.

757
01:10:43,238 --> 01:10:58,982
And it's been very difficult to do what the HRC has done in the past or to do new things or to be responsive to what the community wants with too few members engaged fully.

758
01:11:02,385 --> 01:11:12,070
It's exciting to see so many gifted people and committed Medford residents who wanna participate in this.

759
01:11:12,930 --> 01:11:14,791
And I think you've come at the right time.

760
01:11:17,172 --> 01:11:21,314
Neil Osborne used to say all the time, the commission is what the commission is make of it.

761
01:11:22,395 --> 01:11:28,778
There are constraints, but the commissioners can push against those constraints.

762
01:11:30,147 --> 01:11:39,112
So it's up to the members of this body to really decide the future of the commission.

763
01:11:39,852 --> 01:11:44,615
This subcommittee that I'm on and others are on will offer recommendations.

764
01:11:46,095 --> 01:11:49,857
So I think you've come at a great time, but it is a time of flux.

765
01:11:50,058 --> 01:11:54,600
You have to bear with uncertainty for a certain amount of time.

766
01:11:54,620 --> 01:11:54,680
And

767
01:11:57,671 --> 01:12:18,480
You know, my hope is that your energy that I've seen here tonight and the experience that you have, the passion that you have for these issues can really be harnessed and used by the HRC to address the big issues that we face as a city.

768
01:12:19,681 --> 01:12:25,624
So I'm excited and I think it's gonna be hard work for the next several months.

769
01:12:28,382 --> 01:12:43,132
But just looking at you and seeing the work that several folks have done, particularly Francis, to recruit people is very encouraging.

770
01:12:43,953 --> 01:12:52,979
And I know the commissioners who've been here are very excited to see a great group of people.

771
01:12:53,680 --> 01:12:57,222
And I'm just hoping we can hang in there

772
01:12:58,468 --> 01:12:59,789
It's not gonna happen overnight.

773
01:13:01,009 --> 01:13:02,730
Many of us are learning how to do this.

774
01:13:03,390 --> 01:13:11,753
We're new to the city or new to this work or new to a quasi city kind of body.

775
01:13:12,693 --> 01:13:18,135
But I'm just hopeful and thankful to see you all.

776
01:13:23,717 --> 01:13:26,198
Okay, I guess I'll ask Judy.

777
01:13:28,403 --> 01:13:30,324
Um, yeah, I agree.

778
01:13:30,544 --> 01:13:32,845
And then we have two additional people.

779
01:13:32,965 --> 01:13:40,348
So I, I think it's, it's the start of a new era for the Medford human rights commission.

780
01:13:40,668 --> 01:13:57,594
Um, I would be interested if in, um, Going in hearing about and understanding better the process that we're going to use to, um, name the five people.

781
01:13:58,328 --> 01:13:59,069
Who will join.

782
01:13:59,089 --> 01:14:01,532
So I guess that's it.

783
01:14:01,672 --> 01:14:02,633
Yeah.

784
01:14:03,273 --> 01:14:08,539
Yeah, so certainly I mean it's discussed over, you know, several meetings.

785
01:14:09,640 --> 01:14:16,808
What was important for me was to make sure that if we have vacancies on any commission, right.

786
01:14:17,669 --> 01:14:18,770
I'm reviewing.

787
01:14:19,890 --> 01:14:21,111
applicants, right?

788
01:14:21,211 --> 01:14:23,713
Like somebody, because the applications don't come to me.

789
01:14:24,253 --> 01:14:29,676
So somebody needs to say, Francis, X number of people have applied.

790
01:14:30,197 --> 01:14:37,982
So what happened was I was just asking randomly, like, how come the Human Rights Commission just can't be full?

791
01:14:38,042 --> 01:14:39,543
Where are these applicants?

792
01:14:39,803 --> 01:14:46,067
And I was like, received a pile of applications.

793
01:14:46,899 --> 01:14:55,450
some dating too far back to appear serious in any way, shape or form.

794
01:14:56,131 --> 01:14:57,733
And that was disappointing to me.

795
01:14:57,753 --> 01:15:03,180
And I says, okay, so I came on in March, I want anything that started from January of this year.

796
01:15:04,254 --> 01:15:06,316
anybody that sent in.

797
01:15:06,416 --> 01:15:09,118
And some people sent in to multiple commissions.

798
01:15:09,198 --> 01:15:12,201
So it's not just some people applied to human rights.

799
01:15:12,821 --> 01:15:18,846
I'm just going to say a liquor commission, environmental commission, you know, and that is fine.

800
01:15:18,866 --> 01:15:21,969
But I just pulled the ones that were relevant.

801
01:15:22,569 --> 01:15:25,632
And I believe that there were about 17 of them.

802
01:15:26,953 --> 01:15:32,735
And we've been we've had this conversation over multiple months, you know, how is this decision made.

803
01:15:33,115 --> 01:15:44,000
I don't see this as my decision I see any committee needs to be reflective of what that committee is supposed to do to Human Rights Commission.

804
01:15:44,820 --> 01:15:46,921
is for the city of Medford.

805
01:15:47,261 --> 01:15:49,522
It needs to be reflective of Medford.

806
01:15:49,822 --> 01:15:52,002
Medford's voice, Medford's needs.

807
01:15:52,323 --> 01:15:58,605
Not one person, it's not political, it is helping to move Medford along.

808
01:15:58,645 --> 01:16:01,586
And I have actually asked, where are our youth voices?

809
01:16:02,046 --> 01:16:05,747
Now remember, I don't have municipal experience, I came from the state.

810
01:16:06,567 --> 01:16:10,632
And it's like, oh, they were working on a youth ordinance or something.

811
01:16:10,652 --> 01:16:18,262
And then, you know, we all know what happens with the corona visiting and, you know, upending everything.

812
01:16:19,363 --> 01:16:25,591
But I presented to you all that I would like to invite people, I think that

813
01:16:26,132 --> 01:16:40,265
applicants need to have an opportunity to express their interest, to share about their passion, to meet, to attend a couple of meetings, see how these meetings are.

814
01:16:41,626 --> 01:16:52,276
And then commissioners need the opportunity to say, yes, we've been given new breath.

815
01:16:53,892 --> 01:17:05,455
Yes, you know this person is going to be great for this vacancy because when we look at us, you know, none of us, you know, have the skill set in this area in everywhere I go somebody says this is a need.

816
01:17:06,055 --> 01:17:11,576
And that's a conversation that you have and you make recommendations, not the final decision maker.

817
01:17:11,956 --> 01:17:14,717
I take those recommendations and I present them to the mayor.

818
01:17:16,946 --> 01:17:26,700
But I cannot, um, yeah, I don't think that we, how it was explained to me how it was done before I will just say did not sit right with me.

819
01:17:27,201 --> 01:17:29,104
That's not how a committee is comprised.

820
01:17:29,444 --> 01:17:31,387
I told you I do things a little differently.

821
01:17:33,484 --> 01:17:36,266
You know, this is, I mean, I came from human rights.

822
01:17:36,646 --> 01:17:37,707
This is a passion.

823
01:17:38,067 --> 01:17:40,308
Human rights is very, very broad.

824
01:17:40,769 --> 01:17:50,095
The person that I thought that I wanted to be 20 years ago is very different from the person that I am today, you know, and that changes and that evolves.

825
01:17:50,395 --> 01:17:57,539
And committees like this are essential in helping with that evolution and showing us areas.

826
01:17:57,579 --> 01:18:00,041
I mean, this committee was a great support for me.

827
01:18:00,101 --> 01:18:02,323
Imagine coming to a new town and now,

828
01:18:03,183 --> 01:18:03,904
don't live here.

829
01:18:04,184 --> 01:18:06,487
And everybody wasn't nice.

830
01:18:10,051 --> 01:18:20,783
But I had members of this committee to reach out to or who invited me to different places, or who said, hey, do you know, this is happening.

831
01:18:21,724 --> 01:18:21,945
So

832
01:18:25,124 --> 01:18:27,205
you will make your recommendations.

833
01:18:27,705 --> 01:18:35,008
I mean, there was 17 people who all received the email.

834
01:18:35,428 --> 01:18:37,029
Commissioners, correct me if I'm wrong.

835
01:18:37,049 --> 01:18:48,933
I think in the email I sent to you, two people were no longer available because one moved out of town and the other person actually started taking classes that unfortunately meets at this time.

836
01:18:50,339 --> 01:18:58,583
You know, and then everybody else received the information to notified me by email this stuck at work could not get out of work.

837
01:19:00,204 --> 01:19:01,525
And the others.

838
01:19:02,525 --> 01:19:06,407
Nine people showed up today.

839
01:19:06,907 --> 01:19:10,729
So we will have another meeting but it will be up to you commissioners to.

840
01:19:12,958 --> 01:19:15,580
make your suggestions for the spots.

841
01:19:16,121 --> 01:19:25,689
And if you also remember I spoke concurrently about taking a look at our ordinance just because something has been done the same way, does not mean it has to continue.

842
01:19:26,150 --> 01:19:32,756
You know, I am willing to, you know, book that time with city council but we need to be able to say, Hi,

843
01:19:34,517 --> 01:19:37,219
These are the challenges that we have faced.

844
01:19:37,780 --> 01:19:46,366
And we think that by making these amendments, it is going to improve, but we can't do it on a one-off.

845
01:19:46,486 --> 01:19:50,309
It has to be something concrete and something foundational.

846
01:19:57,134 --> 01:20:02,658
Well, I just want to say, in my earlier

847
01:20:03,101 --> 01:20:25,882
years on the commission, when we would have a slot for a new commissioner, we would actually, Neil Osborne, who was in, as everyone knows, Francis's position before, would select

848
01:20:27,248 --> 01:20:44,773
somebody if we had two or three openings he would select exactly that number of people and invite them to join the meetings for a couple meetings and then we would invariably welcome them to the commission.

849
01:20:45,573 --> 01:20:50,995
So this is a different process where we have more people than we have slots for.

850
01:20:52,200 --> 01:21:05,718
So I feel a little bit at sea about how, you know, we're going to make those decisions because every meeting that we have has to be an open meeting, which is

851
01:21:08,646 --> 01:21:13,189
And we can make those decisions as part of an open meeting.

852
01:21:13,650 --> 01:21:20,134
I mean, as somebody who comes from really about DEI, there is no equity in me selecting.

853
01:21:20,574 --> 01:21:22,436
I'm selecting based on my thought.

854
01:21:22,876 --> 01:21:25,258
My thought is not the thought of Medford.

855
01:21:25,678 --> 01:21:28,240
My job is to help Medford move forward.

856
01:21:28,780 --> 01:21:30,762
And so I cannot embody

857
01:21:31,582 --> 01:21:38,566
things and experiences and practices that I know are not going to yield the best result.

858
01:21:39,346 --> 01:21:41,047
What am I selecting based on?

859
01:21:41,147 --> 01:21:48,411
I'm not saying that it's wrong, but I personally couldn't stand by that.

860
01:21:48,431 --> 01:21:49,552
17 people applied.

861
01:21:50,052 --> 01:21:50,652
Who am I?

862
01:21:50,793 --> 01:21:56,696
One person that still needs their GPS to get around Medford, unless you're talking about a restaurant.

863
01:21:57,739 --> 01:22:01,662
Okay, I can find every good restaurant in Bedford, right?

864
01:22:02,582 --> 01:22:05,124
To say, all right, commissioners, I'm giving you this.

865
01:22:05,544 --> 01:22:11,908
It is, we're talking about equitable and inclusive spaces and inclusive voices.

866
01:22:12,849 --> 01:22:19,873
